1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,030
In this video, I'm going to talk

2
00:00:03,030 --> 00:00:05,055
about C dependency generation.

3
00:00:05,055 --> 00:00:07,560
This is a sequence of videos
that'll talk about how to

4
00:00:07,560 --> 00:00:11,340
do dependency generation
for C projects.

5
00:00:11,340 --> 00:00:14,025
I'm going to start by
talking about GNU make.

6
00:00:14,025 --> 00:00:15,780
We're going to
learn how to handle

7
00:00:15,780 --> 00:00:18,240
incremental builds using
GNU make and how to

8
00:00:18,240 --> 00:00:20,850
automate dependency
tracking with

9
00:00:20,850 --> 00:00:23,980
makefile generation
using the GNU make.

10
00:00:25,250 --> 00:00:29,445
First of all, why do we need
to worry about dependencies?

11
00:00:29,445 --> 00:00:33,960
Well, when a C source file
references header files,

12
00:00:33,960 --> 00:00:36,495
then there's a chance
that you need to rebuild

13
00:00:36,495 --> 00:00:38,130
the object file when either the

14
00:00:38,130 --> 00:00:41,295
header file or the
source file changes.

15
00:00:41,295 --> 00:00:44,300
One way that you can
do this is by adding

16
00:00:44,300 --> 00:00:46,850
header files as prerequisites
to your make targets.

17
00:00:46,850 --> 00:00:48,155
If you only have

18
00:00:48,155 --> 00:00:51,425
a few C and a few H
files in your project,

19
00:00:51,425 --> 00:00:53,750
it's pretty easy to just always

20
00:00:53,750 --> 00:00:56,510
make the build depend
on any change to any of

21
00:00:56,510 --> 00:00:59,390
the files and to

22
00:00:59,390 --> 00:01:01,490
track for specific files

23
00:01:01,490 --> 00:01:03,970
which ones include
which header files.

24
00:01:03,970 --> 00:01:06,165
However, once you get to

25
00:01:06,165 --> 00:01:08,585
pretty much any
reasonably sized project,

26
00:01:08,585 --> 00:01:10,010
that becomes pretty difficult to

27
00:01:10,010 --> 00:01:11,645
track and to keep track of

28
00:01:11,645 --> 00:01:14,120
and understanding
from header files

29
00:01:14,120 --> 00:01:15,725
that include other header files,

30
00:01:15,725 --> 00:01:17,300
getting all those
dependencies right

31
00:01:17,300 --> 00:01:18,950
and tracking it manually would

32
00:01:18,950 --> 00:01:20,120
be very error prone and

33
00:01:20,120 --> 00:01:22,780
tedious and not something
you'd ever want to do.

34
00:01:22,780 --> 00:01:24,350
What you can do instead,

35
00:01:24,350 --> 00:01:26,255
if you're planning
to use GNU make,

36
00:01:26,255 --> 00:01:28,940
is you can use GCC plus GNU

37
00:01:28,940 --> 00:01:32,465
make to automatically generate
dependency files for you.

38
00:01:32,465 --> 00:01:34,955
Here's an example of what I mean

39
00:01:34,955 --> 00:01:37,285
when I'm talking about
dependency generation.

40
00:01:37,285 --> 00:01:39,590
If you take a really
simple example

41
00:01:39,590 --> 00:01:41,630
where you have two
different files,

42
00:01:41,630 --> 00:01:43,430
file 1 and 2 dot c,

43
00:01:43,430 --> 00:01:45,500
that each have their
own header file

44
00:01:45,500 --> 00:01:47,060
and then you have
a main file that

45
00:01:47,060 --> 00:01:51,225
calls functions in each one
of those separate C files.

46
00:01:51,225 --> 00:01:53,030
It'll include file 1,

47
00:01:53,030 --> 00:01:55,250
file 2 and it'll call file

48
00:01:55,250 --> 00:01:57,770
1 and file 2 hello
functions two,

49
00:01:57,770 --> 00:02:00,500
in this case, just print
a simple Hello World

50
00:02:00,500 --> 00:02:02,185
from each of the files.

51
00:02:02,185 --> 00:02:04,920
If anything changes
about the file

52
00:02:04,920 --> 00:02:07,400
1 or file 2 header file,

53
00:02:07,400 --> 00:02:09,170
we need to recompile
the main file.

54
00:02:09,170 --> 00:02:10,550
The way we know that is

55
00:02:10,550 --> 00:02:14,000
because the main file
includes both of those files.

56
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:16,160
In other words, we have
a dependency between

57
00:02:16,160 --> 00:02:18,595
the main.c and either
one of the header files.

58
00:02:18,595 --> 00:02:22,190
However, if we look at
file 1 and file 2 dot c,

59
00:02:22,190 --> 00:02:23,990
we only have
dependencies on file

60
00:02:23,990 --> 00:02:25,910
1 or file 2 dot c header files.

61
00:02:25,910 --> 00:02:27,170
We do not need to rebuild

62
00:02:27,170 --> 00:02:31,205
these if the opposite
header file changes.

63
00:02:31,205 --> 00:02:34,180
We can use GCC and GNU make

64
00:02:34,180 --> 00:02:37,825
to automatically build
dependencies for us.

65
00:02:37,825 --> 00:02:40,280
GCC can output GNU make

66
00:02:40,280 --> 00:02:42,395
compatible dependency files with

67
00:02:42,395 --> 00:02:44,795
a few command options
that are available.

68
00:02:44,795 --> 00:02:48,505
Dash MMD will generate the
dependency files for us,

69
00:02:48,505 --> 00:02:51,470
dash MT sets the target name
of the dependency files and

70
00:02:51,470 --> 00:02:54,770
dash MF tells it where
to write the files.

71
00:02:54,770 --> 00:02:57,380
This was not an
original feature of GCC

72
00:02:57,380 --> 00:02:59,450
before and you might see this

73
00:02:59,450 --> 00:03:01,840
if you look at some
older projects.

74
00:03:01,840 --> 00:03:04,219
This was previously
done with sed commands,

75
00:03:04,219 --> 00:03:06,860
where sed is a stream
editor that's able

76
00:03:06,860 --> 00:03:11,015
to generate dependencies
based on text manipulation.

77
00:03:11,015 --> 00:03:13,760
If you see that implemented

78
00:03:13,760 --> 00:03:15,450
on an old project it
was probably done

79
00:03:15,450 --> 00:03:20,590
before GNU make had
support for these flags.

80
00:03:20,840 --> 00:03:24,200
Here's an example of
the dependency files

81
00:03:24,200 --> 00:03:26,330
that GCC can generate for us.

82
00:03:26,330 --> 00:03:30,275
If I look at these
file dot ds files,

83
00:03:30,275 --> 00:03:33,500
what I can see is that
I've got a dot o that's

84
00:03:33,500 --> 00:03:37,500
dependent on either a
dot c or a dot h file.

85
00:03:37,600 --> 00:03:40,310
I can see that the map here for

86
00:03:40,310 --> 00:03:42,560
file 1 and file 2 dot o matches

87
00:03:42,560 --> 00:03:44,375
the header files
that I've already

88
00:03:44,375 --> 00:03:47,045
referenced in each
one of those files.

89
00:03:47,045 --> 00:03:48,815
Similarly for main dot o,

90
00:03:48,815 --> 00:03:50,390
I'm dependent on main C,

91
00:03:50,390 --> 00:03:51,710
file one h and file two

92
00:03:51,710 --> 00:03:53,900
h. Each one of these
were automatically

93
00:03:53,900 --> 00:03:59,040
generated for me based
on the arguments to GCC.

94
00:03:59,870 --> 00:04:02,600
How can you make this a part of

95
00:04:02,600 --> 00:04:05,215
your overall make
and build process?

96
00:04:05,215 --> 00:04:07,670
It's a little bit
complicated to setup.

97
00:04:07,670 --> 00:04:08,810
There's a couple of

98
00:04:08,810 --> 00:04:10,280
links that I've
included here and I've

99
00:04:10,280 --> 00:04:12,170
got an actual working example

100
00:04:12,170 --> 00:04:13,730
if you want to go through it.

101
00:04:13,730 --> 00:04:16,010
I've annotated the steps here

102
00:04:16,010 --> 00:04:19,880
but you can do this
in a generic way

103
00:04:19,880 --> 00:04:22,250
using the framework that
I've provided here in

104
00:04:22,250 --> 00:04:25,054
the automatic
template generation

105
00:04:25,054 --> 00:04:27,094
or dependency
generation example,

106
00:04:27,094 --> 00:04:29,509
where you could just
list source files

107
00:04:29,509 --> 00:04:32,735
and list the application
that you're going to

108
00:04:32,735 --> 00:04:35,510
build and the rest of the
macros here in the makefile

109
00:04:35,510 --> 00:04:36,830
will take care of automatically

110
00:04:36,830 --> 00:04:38,600
generating dependencies for you.

111
00:04:38,600 --> 00:04:40,070
It's using a rule here

112
00:04:40,070 --> 00:04:42,680
to automatically create
the object files from

113
00:04:42,680 --> 00:04:45,110
the sources and
define the all in

114
00:04:45,110 --> 00:04:46,640
default build types based on

115
00:04:46,640 --> 00:04:49,070
the app time that
we specified here.

116
00:04:49,070 --> 00:04:53,300
This command is going to do
the link step for us to take

117
00:04:53,300 --> 00:04:55,250
all the object files and

118
00:04:55,250 --> 00:04:58,420
build them into the app
that we've specified.

119
00:04:58,420 --> 00:05:00,480
In terms of how we actually do

120
00:05:00,480 --> 00:05:01,995
the dependency generation,

121
00:05:01,995 --> 00:05:03,575
we're going to create

122
00:05:03,575 --> 00:05:04,970
a hidden directory where

123
00:05:04,970 --> 00:05:06,625
we'll put all our
dependency files.

124
00:05:06,625 --> 00:05:09,050
A benefit of making a
hidden directory here is

125
00:05:09,050 --> 00:05:12,000
that we typically wouldn't
be looking at these files,

126
00:05:12,000 --> 00:05:13,100
we won't want to check them into

127
00:05:13,100 --> 00:05:15,250
revision control, for instance.

128
00:05:15,250 --> 00:05:18,165
Then we'll have depth flags,

129
00:05:18,165 --> 00:05:22,520
a parameter that will
pass to GCC that will use

130
00:05:22,520 --> 00:05:27,365
the empty MMD and MF arguments
to tell it where to write

131
00:05:27,365 --> 00:05:32,790
the auto-generated
dependency files.

132
00:05:32,790 --> 00:05:35,960
Then you can see the
compile C command

133
00:05:35,960 --> 00:05:37,610
that we're specifying here,

134
00:05:37,610 --> 00:05:39,740
just specifies doing
the compile step with

135
00:05:39,740 --> 00:05:41,210
all the dependent flags and

136
00:05:41,210 --> 00:05:43,890
any other flags that
were specified.

137
00:05:44,300 --> 00:05:48,190
Then we can generate
rules here that use

138
00:05:48,190 --> 00:05:51,340
our compile C to generate
the output dot o file

139
00:05:51,340 --> 00:05:53,530
based on both the dependencies

140
00:05:53,530 --> 00:05:55,090
in the dependency
directory that we're

141
00:05:55,090 --> 00:05:57,790
creating plus the dot c file

142
00:05:57,790 --> 00:05:59,710
that we're actually compiling.

143
00:05:59,710 --> 00:06:03,700
Then we'll include all
the dependency files in

144
00:06:03,700 --> 00:06:11,295
the list of files that we
include in our makefile.

145
00:06:11,295 --> 00:06:12,610
That we'll pull in

146
00:06:12,610 --> 00:06:14,230
all those dependencies mentioned

147
00:06:14,230 --> 00:06:15,895
in each one of the
dependency files,

148
00:06:15,895 --> 00:06:18,355
as a part of the rules for doing

149
00:06:18,355 --> 00:06:21,770
make right here with
this wildcard option.

150
00:06:22,580 --> 00:06:25,570
There's a test script that

151
00:06:25,570 --> 00:06:28,490
verifies that only the files you

152
00:06:28,490 --> 00:06:30,875
expect are going
to be recompiled

153
00:06:30,875 --> 00:06:34,220
on when touching one of
the two header files.

154
00:06:34,220 --> 00:06:35,960
You can go through this
if you want without

155
00:06:35,960 --> 00:06:38,980
a make dependency
generation example.

156
00:06:38,980 --> 00:06:41,060
You can definitely do this,

157
00:06:41,060 --> 00:06:42,830
but there are some
pitfalls to be aware of if

158
00:06:42,830 --> 00:06:44,945
you're going to try to
use GNU make for this.

159
00:06:44,945 --> 00:06:48,710
One is dependency
tracking isn't automatic.

160
00:06:48,710 --> 00:06:50,870
The example that I

161
00:06:50,870 --> 00:06:54,080
gave covers most of the
dependencies you'll care about,

162
00:06:54,080 --> 00:06:56,090
but there are still
some that are missing.

163
00:06:56,090 --> 00:06:57,710
For instance, the makefile,

164
00:06:57,710 --> 00:06:59,060
if the makefile changes,

165
00:06:59,060 --> 00:07:01,760
you probably need to
rebuild the entire project.

166
00:07:01,760 --> 00:07:04,100
That's not in the dependencies
that I shared with

167
00:07:04,100 --> 00:07:08,100
that simple example and I'm
sure there are other ones.

168
00:07:08,770 --> 00:07:11,930
Also the makefile
implementation details

169
00:07:11,930 --> 00:07:14,225
are probably platform specific.

170
00:07:14,225 --> 00:07:17,645
Definitely the case that I
just showed is GCC specific,

171
00:07:17,645 --> 00:07:20,230
it's only going to work
with the GCC compiler.

172
00:07:20,230 --> 00:07:22,759
It assumes that binary utilities

173
00:07:22,759 --> 00:07:25,325
are present in the
root file system.

174
00:07:25,325 --> 00:07:28,700
The utilities that are using for

175
00:07:28,700 --> 00:07:32,425
the dependency generation
step have to be installed.

176
00:07:32,425 --> 00:07:34,430
It probably means that your code

177
00:07:34,430 --> 00:07:36,365
won't build on other platforms.

178
00:07:36,365 --> 00:07:39,840
For instance, one that
didn't have GCC installed.